==English==
{{was wotd|2020|December|3}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂welh₁-|id=rule}}
From {{inh|en|enm|vailaunt|t=having or showing courage or valour, valiant; characterized by valour; powerful, strong; person of valour or strength; excellent, worthy; beneficial, useful; valuable; legally valid, binding}}{{nb...|vailant, vailaunt, vailiant, vaillant, vaillaunt, vaillaunte, valiant, valiante, valiaunte, valliaunt, valyant, valyante, valyaunt, valyaunte, vaylaunt, waillant, (chiefly Scotland) vailȝeand|otherforms=1}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=vailaunt|pos=adj|id=MED50690}}</ref> from {{der|en|xno|vaillaunt}}, {{m|xno|vaylant}}{{nb...|vailliant, wallant|otherforms=1}}, and {{der|en|fro|vailant}}, {{m|fro|vaillant|t=brave, valiant; having value, valuable}}{{nb...|vailand, vailent|otherforms=1}}, from the {{glossary|present}} {{glossary|participle}} of {{m|fro|valoir|t=to have value; to be worth}}, from {{der|en|la|valeō|valēre|t=to have value; to be worth; to be strong; to have influence or power}},<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''adj.'' (and ''n.'')|noformat=1|id=221186|date=1916|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=adj}}</ref> ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₂welh₁-|t=powerful, strong; to rule}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈvæliənt/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-valiant.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{hyphenation|en|va|liant}}, {{hyphenation|en|va|li|ant|nocaption=1}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[possess|Possessing]] or [[show#Verb|showing]] [[courage]] or [[determination]]; [[brave#Adjective|brave]], [[heroic]].
#: {{synonyms|en|bold|valorous|Thesaurus:brave}}
#: {{antonyms|en|Thesaurus:cowardly}}
#: {{ux|en|A '''valiant''' man’s look is more than a coward’s sword.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Thomas Cooper (bishop)|Thomas Cooper]]|chapter=To the Ryght Honorable Lorde Russell Erle of Bedforde, and One of the Queenes Maiesties Moste Honorable Counsell: Thomas Cooper Wisheth Longe Continuance of Prosperous Life and Muche Honour.|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=sSVQAQAAIAAJ&pg=PA7|title=Coopers Chronicle, Conteininge the Whole Discourse of the Histories as well of This Realme, as All Other Countreis,{{nb...|with the Sucession of Their Kynges, the Time of Their Raigne, and what Notable Actes were Done by Them, Newly Enlarged and Augmented, as well in the First Part, with Diuers Profitable Histories, as in the Latter Ende with the Whole Summe of Those Thinges that Paulus Iouius and Sleidane hath Written of Later Yeres, that is, from the Beginnyng of Kyng Henrie the Eightes Raigne vnto the Late Death of Queene Marie, by Me Thomas Cooper}}|edition=new|location=London|publisher={{...|[in Fletestrete,]}} &#91;in the house late [[w:Thomas Berthelet|Thomas Berthelettes]]&#93;|year=1560|oclc=1172192520|passage=For by readyng of hiſtories fyrſte we know how longe time mightie empyres, great kyngedomes, famous common weales and citees haue floriſhed: how many yeres noble prynces, '''valiant''' capitaynes, and wyſe gouernours haue reigned: in what age they were, which was before other, and how farre diſtante in tyme one from an other.}}
#* {{RQ:Geneva Bible|1 Chronicles|XII|8|column=2|passage=And of the Gadites there ſeparated them ſelues ſome vnto [[w:David|Dauid]] into the holde of the wildernes, '''valiant''' men of warre, ''and'' mẽ of armes, & ''apt'' for battel, which colde handle ſpeare and ſhield, and their faces ''were like'' the faces of lyons, and ''were'' like the roes in the mountaines in ſwiftenes, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Timon of Athens|act=III|scene=v|page=88|column=1|passage=Hee's truly '''Valiant''', that can wiſely ſuffer / The worſt that man can breath, / And make his Wrongs, his Out-ſides, / To weare them like his Rayment, careleſſely, / And ne're preferre his iniuries to his heart, / To bring it into danger.}}
#* {{RQ:Bunyan Pilgrim's Progress|page=61|passage=Then they read to him ſome of the worthy Acts that ſome of his Servants had done. As how they had ſubdued Kingdoms, wrought Righteouſneſs, obtained Promiſes, ſtopped the mouths of Lions, quenched the violence of Fire, eſcaped the edge of the Sword; out of weakneſs were made ſtrong, waxed '''valiant''' in fight, and turned to flight the Armies of the ''Aliens''. &#91;[[w:Epistle to the Hebrews|Heb[rews]]] 11. 33, 34.&#93;}}
#* {{RQ:L'Estrange Fables of Aesop|fablenumber=CCVII|fable=A Wolfe and a Kid|page=178|passage=The Advantages of Time and Place are enough to make a Poultron '''Valiant'''. There's Nothing ſo Couragious as a Coward if you put him out of Danger.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[Maurice Shelton or John Randall]|chapter=Of the First ''Roman'' Nobility|title=An Historical and Critical Essay on the True Rise of Nobility, Political and Civil;{{nb...|from the First Ages of the World, thro the Jewish, Grecian, Roman Commonwealths, &c. down to this Present Time. To which is Annex’d, the Order of Precedency; with Other Curious Things: Chiefly Extracted from a Valuable Manuscript, Writ by a Herald [i.e., Robert Brown]. With a Compleat Index to the Whole.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} [[w:Charles Rivington|C[harles] Rivington]]{{nb...|at the Bible and Crown in St. Paul’s Church-yard.}}|year=1718|page=51|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=EpdcAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA51|oclc=776757599|passage=But admit that good Men are begotten of good Parents, and '''valiant''' Men of '''valiant''' Fathers; and if this be to Mankind proper, why are not good and '''valiant''' Children begotten of good and '''valiant''' common Perſons also? for they are Men as well as the other. But nothing is more deceitful than this Rule; for through the Corruption of Man's Nature we ſee it often happens, a prodigal Son to be born of a frugal Father, a Fool of a wiſe Man, and a Coward of a courageous Man.}}
# [[characterize|Characterized]] by or [[do#Verb|done]] with [[bravery]] or [[valour]].
#: {{ux|en|another valiant attempt at rescue}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Troilus and Cressida|act=II|scene=ii|page=37|column=1|passage=She is a theame of honour and renowne, / A ſpurre to '''valiant''' and magnanimous deeds, / Whoſe preſent courage may beate downe our foes, / And fame in time to come canonize us, [...]}}

