===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[person]] who [[act#Verb|acts]] with [[valour]], [[show#Verb|showing]] [[hero]][[-like]] [[characteristic#Noun|characteristics]] in the midst of [[danger]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Julius Caesar|act=II|scene=ii|page=117|column=1|passage=Cowards dye many times before their deaths, / The '''valiant''' neuer taſte of death but once: [...]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=“Ch. M.” [pseudonym; {{w|Matthew Kellison}}]|chapter=The Tenth Flovver of the Myrrhine Posie: Christ Crvcified: Or His Sufferances on the Crosse.{{nb...|Mat[thew] 27. Mar[k] 15. Lu[ke] 23. Ioa. [i.e., John] 19.}}|title=A Myrrhine Posie of the Bitter Dolovrs of Christ: His Passion, and of the Seaven VVords He Spake on the Crosse|location=Doway [''i.e.'', Douai, France]|publisher={{...|Printed at Doway by}} L. Kellam|year=1639|page=123|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=hPPLRRjMWbYC&pg=PA123|oclc=1203809202|passage=O yee Angells, yee Champions and '''valiants''' of the court of heauen, and ſtout ſoldiers of Chriſt your King, who euerie one ſingle is able to encounter and to defeat the greateſt armie that euer was ſeene on earth, where are you?}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Absalom|part=2|page=32|passage=No Sailer vvith the Nevvs ſvvell ''Egypt''’s Pride. / By vvhat inglorious Fate our '''Valiant''' dy’d!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Plautus|chapter=[[w:Truculentus|The Churl]]|translator=&#91;[[w:Richard Warner (botanist)|Richard Warner]]&#93;|title=Comedies of Plautus, Translated into Familiar Blank Verse,{{nb...|by the Gentleman who Translated The Captives.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} T. Becket and P. A. de Hondt,{{nb...|in the Strand.}}|year=1772|volume=IV|section=act II, scene ii|page=228|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=A4QrAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA228|lines=19–22|oclc=504223901|passage=The '''valiant''' profit more / Their country, than the fineſt clevereſt ſpeakers. / Valour once known, will ſoon find eloquence / To trumpet forth her praiſe— {{...}}}}

